His son, named Aṃśumat, was endowed with all good qualities;
he pleased his father and his father's father, the ruler of the earth.

Sītāvaktra and the Sindhu went facing the west;
and toward the south went Gaṅgā, following Bhagīratha's path,
here with great velocity, there creeping slowly,

Cyavana said:
"This very thing will surely come to pass, which you ask of me, O king;
the status of a brahmin will belong to you through the lineage following our lineage.

Sūrya said:
"This is my inherent nature, O brahmin, that my essence is heat;
and since I act according to my nature, you ought not to be angry with me.

Rāhu said:
Enmity with the all-powerful Viṣṇu does not please me,
from whose wrath I was born without a body, the crusher of the enemy's host,

The Holy One said:
O thirty [gods], you ought to make an effort in churning the Ocean of Milk;
joined with the dānavas, you will then attain the highest good.

Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Adorned with a matted coil of hair resembling the color of fire flames,
as if the sun's orb in summer had mounted half the sky;

Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Learn from me the gotra-founders born in the lineage of Atri:
The Karpūrāyaṇas, the Śākheyas, and those who are Śārāhaṇas;
Śaivālakī, Śaunavarṇi, and those who are the Aghosaudrutis;

On the back of that peaked mountain is a lake with fully blown lotuses,
crowded with kāraṇḍava ducks, and adorned by royal swans,
deep like the mind of the virtuous, pure like the mind of the virtuous,
serving many uses, and bringing release to the good, O king;
with fish like severed pieces of cloud, with blooming white water-lilies like stars,
with the great night-lotus like the lord of the night, like a second sky;
where the excellent lotuses were covered all around by honeybees
yellowed with the pollen of full-blown lotuses, as if [made] of refined gold;
where the water and lotuses were hard to distinguish on account of pollen fallen from lotuses
and churned from filaments by swans, swirled about by the wind;
where large lotus leaves, accompanied by drops from clouds shaken down by the wind,
shine by the thousands, resembling pearls;
where lotuses dance, struck by the darting of small carp,
resounding with the calls of noisy cranes and the hum of bees singing aloud;
as if laughing through flocks of swans, as if dancing through waves,
as if chanting through the auspicious cries of kāraṇḍava ducks;

Aparājita said:
O king, a fearless man finds everything in the arms of the brave;
therefore, the destruction of the descendants of Raghu through valor pleases me.

Ajagara said:
Action for decoration brings prosperity when one has not conquered hosts of foes;
calamity is preferable to those decorations of prosperity that are not born of conquest.

Ruha said:
Fierce heroes look magnificent, like swelling breasts;
and for heroes as for breasts, battle brings scorn on the enemy.

Gadyu said:
Let weapons, broken and laughing mockingly when consumed, remain useless;
even if only one staff-like arm remains, who would tolerate defeat?

