may these deities named the Bhṛgus consecrate you;
Manas, Mantṛ, Prāṇa, Nara, the mighty Apāna,
Vinirbhaya, Naya, Haṃsa, Nārāyaṇa,

Loss of harsh instruments is not desired for rulers of the earth. / Whatever task someone may have special skill in,
to that task the king should assign him after testing. / He should employ servants descended from his fathers and grandfathers in all tasks,
except in affairs concerning heirs; for there, in brief, / the king, having tested men whom he has himself advanced, in affairs concerning heirs,
should appoint them, O fortunate one; they will act for his welfare. / With the desire to win over people, those who have come from another king's court,
whether upright or wicked, he should carefully give refuge to. / Having known someone to be wicked, the lord of the earth should not place trust in him;
yet he should grant him a livelihood, with the desire to win over people. / A king should greatly honor a man who has arrived from another country.
He should consider an ally who has arrived from [his own] land with high regard. / At will, a king should not gather an excessive number of servants, O foremost of the Bhṛgus;

even when not angry, he appears enraged; even when pleased, he is fruitless; / he speaks slowly and cuts off one's livelihood;
he does not honor [the dependent] when words of admittance are spoken; / and in all acts of adoration, he behaves as if asleep;
in conversations, he reproaches with faults and interrupts speech; / and he is seen turning away when one's virtues are praised;
and when work is being done, he turns his gaze elsewhere. / Having heard the sign of one alienated, listen to the sign of an affectionate [king]:
upon seeing [him], he becomes pleased and receives his word with respect; / in inquiries about well-being, he offers a seat;
he does not suspect him in private audience and in secret; / his face becomes delighted upon hearing his conversation;
he rejoices even in unpleasant words spoken by him; / and he receives a present, even a slight one, with respect;

he scratches the ground, he shakes his neck, O twice-born, / he scratches his head, and he licks his lower lip;
he acts hastily in tasks, O Rāma, even when they are perverse. / Such signs of a poisoner one should examine.
Then, acting swiftly, he should test that food in fire: / [if it has] the colour of a rainbow, accompanied by cracking like wood,
swirling in a single whorl, foul-smelling, and crackling violently— / and from inhaling its smoke a creature develops a headache.
Flies do not alight on poisoned food, O descendant of Bhṛgu; / and those that do alight perish. When poison is seen,
the gaze of the cakora bird loses its colour, O best of the Bhṛgus; / and the voice of the cuckoo becomes unnatural, O twice-born;
the gait of the swan falters, and the bhṛṅgarāja bird cries out; / the krauñca falls into excitement, and the rooster shrieks;
the cakora bird screams, and the starling shrieks; / the goldcrest turns away, and the kāraṇḍava duck perishes;

the monkey urinates, O Rāma, and the jīvañjīvaka bird faints; / the babhru mongoose's hair stands on end, and the spotted deer weeps;
and the peacock rejoices at the sight of poison, O twice-born. / Poisoned food, O Rāma, takes a long time to cook,
and it becomes excessively runny, cooked yet like stale food, / with ruined flavour and scent, and covered with spots resembling moonbeams.
Cooked dishes become dry, bubbles arise in liquids, / and a line of foam appears in salty preparations;
in meat-broth there is a blue streak, in milk a coppery one, / and in curds and water it resembles the colour of a cuckoo, O foremost of the Bhṛgus;
in fermented gruel it is black, in kodo millet reddish-brown, / in buttermilk it is brownish-grey like honey, blue, or yellow;
in clarified butter it looks like water, in whey it has the colour of a pigeon, / in honey it is green, and in oil it is reddish;
unripe fruits ripen swiftly, / while ripe ones spoil, and garlands wither;

hard things become soft, and soft things the reverse; / fine threads stick together, and hairiness appears on them;
dark circular marks form, especially on clothes; / and metals and jewels are covered with dirt and slime;
ointments, perfumes, and bath-waters, O foremost of the Bhṛgus, / become foul-smelling, and leaves wither;
the collyrium, O Rāma, becomes yellow, blue, or white; / the bark of the tooth-cleaning stick ceases to yield, and strings appear [on it].
Such signs are to be recognised, O foremost of the Bhṛgus. / Therefore a king should always remain surrounded by gems, mantras, and herbs,
protected, O Rāma, by trustworthy men free from carelessness.
In this world the ruler of the earth is the root of the tree of subjects; through his protection the kingdom thrives. / Therefore the protection of the king must be undertaken with every effort by all, O moon of the Bhṛgu lineage.
Rāma said:
Expound the science of architecture, O son of the king of water creatures, / for the welfare of kings and of others, O bestower of honour.

Puṣkara said:
First one should inspect the land endowed with auspicious marks, / sloping towards the east and north, auspicious, and raised towards the south;
not cut through by gravel, not inundated by water from elsewhere, / free from anthills, mice burrows, chasms, and thorny trees;
soft to the touch, firm, not saline, / not shaped like barley, not resembling an elephant or a thunderbolt,
not shaped like a winnowing basket or a tortoise, not lacking vitality, / not infested with worms, possessing an abundance of soil, and auspicious;
in whose pit a flower does not wither, / and in which a lamp does not go out, O descendant of Bhṛgu;
and in which water is not quickly absorbed, O Rāma— / that earth is praiseworthy; upon it a wise man should establish a dwelling.
White, red, yellow, and black soil respectively / are praiseworthy for brahmins and the other [castes], O twice-born;

and having a smell like clarified butter, blood, grain, or liquor, / and having a sweet, astringent, sour, or pungent taste;
and covered with kuśa, śara, kāśa, or dūrvā grass. / Having carefully examined the land at an auspicious lunar day and asterism,
having first honoured the brahmins, one should clear it of bones and debris; / then, having excavated it, one should assign the portions of the deities.
Having first made the site sixty-four plots according to rule, / having divided it into sixty-four divisions on all sides,
one should assign each house there accordingly; / for the placement of doors is taught according to the division of the town.
Hereafter I shall declare the deities' portions according to rule: / In the centre, the four-plot master is Brahmā, the auspicious four-faced one; / to the east, the master is declared to be Aryaman, the restrainer; / to the south is Vivasvat, and Mitra stands to the west;

to the north is Pṛthvīdhara; now listen to me regarding the intermediate directions: / in the intermediate direction of Śiva, the two gods are Kāma and Pakṣa;
Sāvitrī and Savitṛ are declared in the southeast; / and in the southwest, O Rāma, Jaya and Indra are declared;
Bhadra and Vyādhi are declared in the northwest, O son of Bhṛgu. / Outside these deities, moreover, in the outer ring,
learn from me those deities in the directions, beginning with the east: / Mahendra, Ravi, Satya, and Bhṛśa are proclaimed in the east, O Rāma;
Gṛhakṣita, Yama, Bhṛṅga, and Gandharva in the south; / Bhallāṭa, Soma, Aditi, and Dhanada
[These are] traditionally taught as the deities on the north. Now listen to me [regarding those] in the intermediate corners: / Diti and Īśa, and Megha and Jaya, are proclaimed in the corner of Śiva.
Vyoman and Agni, and Pūṣan and Vitatha [are] in the corner of Agni, O descendant of Bhṛgu. / And in the southwest [are] Mṛga, Pitṛ, Īśa, Dauvārika, and Sugrīva.
Roga, Anāyu, Nāga, and Mukhya stand in the direction of the Wind. / Now listen to me as I declare the eight on the outside even of these:

One should make it there [where it does not] conflict with a solitary tree, O foremost of the twice-born. / Alternatively, house construction is undertaken according to the division of the directions:
the house for the deities in the northeast, and the kitchen in the southeast; / the fire-chamber there as well, and the drain in the southwest;
the storehouse and the armoury are recorded in the northwest. / Water from a well and the like is praised in the northern directions;
in the other [directions] it is censured, O Rāma, and one should avoid it with effort. / In a house, one should avoid timber that is old mixed with new.
One who knows should not build a shared wall or another's wall; / nor, except during an eclipse of the sun or moon, an alteration of the door.
One should not make an expansion or reduction when work on the house is resumed; / or they should be made on the northeast with the consent of brahmins. / The Brahma-place must always be made pure, O descendant of Bhṛgu;
and it should not be obstructed with pegs and the like. / One should know the vital points, O Rāma, at the junctions of the plots of the deities;

because the confluence of all sacred pilgrimage places is in the hand of a brahmin. / From brahmins arose the three worlds, with the moving and the unmoving.
Their curses are proclaimed in the three worlds to be infallible, O knower of dharma; / and the blessing of brahmins is declared to be infallible.
Therefore, the best of the twice-born should be made intent on blessing by the king; / by whom fire was made all-devouring and the great ocean undrinkable,
by whom Soma was made to wane, and Śatakratu given the testicles of a goat, / and the fire of whose anger even today does not subside in Daṇḍaka.
And their favor is vast, O son of Bhṛgu; / by whose favor the moon, even when waned, longs for waxing;
by whose favor the sun is not overcome by the rākṣasas; / by whose favor Kārtavīrya attained vast fortune;
by whose favor Dhanada attained the superintendence of wealth; / the unbathed obtains the merit of bathing, and the vowless obtains a vow;

Worship of Keśava, O Rāma, is nevertheless fruitful for a woman;
Govinda is [the indweller of] all beings, the [true] husband, and none other for a woman.
Therefore, even without the husband's permission, His worship is prescribed.

who, turning her face away, with a faint smile / takes even affectionate words as if unheard, and conceals her hips;
whose face turns pale when he is seen, who turns away even from his friends, / and appears merely indifferent toward the women he loves;
who, even though knowing it is an auspicious time, does not put on adornments— / such a woman is disaffected. Now hear from me as I speak of one who is in love.
Merely upon seeing him she becomes delighted, and looks at him with her face turned away; / when she is looked at, she casts her restless gaze elsewhere;
yet she turns [her gaze] away, but cannot do so completely; / likewise, O descendant of Bhṛgu, she exposes limbs that should be well hidden;
and with effort she conceals an unblemished limb; / on seeing him, she hugs and kisses a child;
when addressed, her speech falters; / when touched, with horripilated limbs, she leans upon her female friends or [slumps] in weariness;
and likewise, O Rāma, she asks for articles that are easily obtained; / then, receiving even a little, she attains the highest joy;

at the mere mention of his name she rejoices and holds it dear; / she also sends him fruits marked with her fingernails;
and what is sent by him she places with reverence upon her heart; / and with her embraces she seems to smear his limbs with nectar;
when he is asleep she sleeps, but goes to sleep after him and wakes before him; / she touches his thighs repeatedly, and kisses him while he sleeps.
Having thus recognised a woman who is in love, a self-possessed man may love her. / Sensual desire, dining, and friendship—dolls should be understood
when one wishes to win over a young girl, along with toys and the like; / by offering perfumes and garlands one should bring a youthful woman under control;
and by giving clothes and ornaments, a woman whose youth has passed. / A young girl is fond of play and good company, and likewise one whose youth has passed;
a young woman is known to be fond of pleasure, or fond of both. / Self-importance should never be displayed before women.
Deep resentment arises toward a man who displays self-importance. / One should neither constantly grant them sight of oneself, nor remain entirely out of sight.

should all be fined double, because of their deception of the king.
If one should say, “I said this to him out of affection, or out of negligence;
and I will not speak thus again,” he should receive half the fine.

For thread, cotton, fermented dregs, cow-dung, and molasses;
for curds, milk, buttermilk, water, and fruit;
for bamboo and split-reed utensils, and likewise salts;
for all earthenware, for earth, and for ash;

those who know dharma and speak the truth declare it a transgression of the vow.
To achieve purification for this very [sin], wearing the skin of a donkey,
he should beg alms at seven houses, proclaiming his own deed;
subsisting once a day on the alms obtained from them,
and bathing at the three junctures of the day, he is purified in a year.
Having killed an unknown fetus, he should perform the observance for killing a brahmin;
as also for [killing] a kṣatriya or vaiśya who has sacrificed, or a woman who has bathed after her courses,
or having spoken falsely in testimony, or having contradicted teachers,
or having misappropriated a deposit, or having killed a woman or a friend.
A twice-born who has drunk liquor through delusion should drink fire-hot liquor;
when his body is consumed by it, he is freed from that sin;
or he should drink fire-hot cow's urine, or water,
or milk, or clarified butter until death, or the juice of cow dung;
or for a year, wearing rags, with matted hair, carrying a staff [topped with a skull],
he should eat broken grains or oil-cake once at night to dispel the drinking of liquor.
Liquor is indeed the impurity of foods, and sin is called impurity;

A thief is purified by death, but a brahmin through asceticism alone.
Wishing to dispel through asceticism the stain arising from the theft of gold,
a twice-born, clad in rags, should perform the observance for a slayer of a brahmin in the forest.
He who has violated his guru's bed, having confessed it, should lie down on an iron bed;
embracing a glowing iron effigy, he is purified by death;
or having himself cut off his penis and testicles and placed them in his cupped hands,
he should walk straight toward the south-west until he falls dead;
or carrying a skull-topped staff, clad in rags, bearded, in a lonely forest,
he should attentively perform the prājāpatya kṛcchra penance for one year;
or with senses restrained, he should practice the cāndrāyaṇa for three months,
with sacrificial food and barley gruel, for the removal of [the sin of] violating the guru's bed.
Having intentionally performed an act causing loss of caste,
he should perform the sāntapana kṛcchra penance, but if unintentionally, the prājāpatya.
In acts leading to a mixed state, the lunar penance is the purification;

or bathe in a flowing river, or chant the threefold hymn. / Having killed a snake, the best of the twice-born should give an iron spade;
for an eunuch, a load of straw and a māṣaka of lead; / for a boar, a pot of ghee; and for a partridge, a droṇa of sesame seeds;
for a parrot, a two-year-old calf; having killed a krauñca, a two-year-old [calf]; / having killed a swan, a crane, a heron, or a peacock,
a monkey, a falcon, or a vulture, he should present a cow to a brahmin. / Having killed a horse, he should give a garment; for an elephant, five dark bulls;
having killed a goat, a sheep, an ox, or a donkey, a one-year-old [calf]; / having killed a carnivorous doe, he should give a milch cow;
for a non-carnivorous one, a heifer; but having killed a camel, a kṛṣṇala. / For [killing] animals such as deer, bow-carrying animals, or calves, he should give each separately for purification.
Having killed unintentionally a woman of any of the four varnas, / [or] of the three varnas in regular order, without distinction,

having bathed and not eaten for one day, greeting respectfully, he should propitiate [him]. / Having struck [him] even with a blade of grass, or bound [him] by the neck with a cloth,
or even having defeated [him] in an argument, bowing down, he should propitiate [him]. / Threatening [a brahmin] with intent to kill, he attains hell for a hundred years,
and for a thousand [years] by striking [him]. / As many dust-particles as the blood of a twice-born binds together,
for that many thousands of years the perpetrator goes to hell. / For threatening, he should perform a kṛcchra, an atikṛcchra for striking down;
he should perform a kṛcchrātikṛcchra upon drawing blood from a brahmin. / A Caṇḍāla or the like, unknown, who should stay in someone's dwelling,
when properly identified in time, he should perform its purification. / A cāndrāyaṇa or a parāka is the purification for the twice-born,
while a prājāpatya is for śūdras, and the rest in accordance with that. / Jaggery, safflower, salt, and whatever grains [there are],
having placed [them] in the house, he should then apply fire to them at the door. / But for earthen vessels, discarding alone is prescribed;

Ending in 'śarman', 'varman', 'dhana' or 'artha', and 'dāsa' respectively,
the name should be given, or in accordance with one's family.
For women, it should be easy to pronounce, not harsh, of clear meaning, and charming;
the name, O noble one, should be given, and it should not have an uneven number of syllables.
Having named the child, he should present him to the mace-bearing God after worship,
O noble one, saying: 'Indeed, this is your son.'
A student, a servant, an attendant, and one who receives a share is Keśava;
Madhusūdana is always present through an auspicious distribution.
Then worship of the brahmins should be performed according to rule;
and here he should feed the brahmins with rice-milk accompanied by a sacrificial fee.
Then the tonsure-rite should be performed, O son of Bhṛgu, according to family custom;
lunar mansions here are recommended that are soft, swift, and movable.
He should avoid an empty lunar day, as well as the days of the Sun and Mars;
having performed worship there especially to Vāsudeva.

Having performed purification in a pure place, facing east, fully composed, / or facing north, knower of dharma, and keeping his hands between his knees,
he should always sip water, knower of dharma, with washed feet and hands; / but not with water poured with one hand, or full of foam and bubbles,
nor indeed with water heated by fire should a wise man sip. / At the tips of the fingers is the place of the gods; at the base of the fingers is that of Brahmā;
at the base of the forefinger is that of the ancestors, and at the base of the little finger is that of men. / With the part sacred to Brahmā he should drink water three times, and then wipe his mouth.
He should next touch the bodily apertures with water, as well as the crown of the head and the navel. / Having sipped water, he should then chew the tooth-cleaning stick;
having chewed it, Rāma, he should always throw it away attentively, / facing north or facing east, never facing the southern direction.
He should not chew meat, nor a stick dry at the top, nor one that is slimy, / nor one that is hollow, sweet, sour, barkless, or with leaves attached;

biting the nails, and grinding the teeth, / and cutting hair with the nails—he should avoid useless actions;
and making sounds with mouth and limbs, or useless whistling and shouting. / Having wrapped his head, he should never sleep at night.
Likewise, O Rāma, he should not wander at night without a lamp; / he should not enter a cattle-shed by a non-door, nor an enemy's house by a non-door;
he should not enter or remain [there], and he should not awaken one who is asleep; / he should not interrupt another's speech, nor alienate one who is attached;
he should not break off a conversation, nor reverse his garments; / he should not stay long with raised knees, nor leap over a jumping [cow];
a cow grazing in another's field or suckling a calf / he should not report to another, nor should he point out a rainbow;
he should say, 'Good, good', and never utter what is unpleasant. / A seat made of palāśa wood is to be avoided, as are a footstool and sandals [of it].

or of the mother's, O best of the descendants of Bhṛgu; and he should perform the śrauta rites in the consecrated fires. / Because a man is born in debt to the gods, / the ancestors, and the sages,
he should always strive for liberation from that [debt]. / A living being becomes free from debt to the gods through sacrifices, O giver of honor,
or, if he has little wealth, through worships, fasts, and vows; / and he becomes free from debt to the ancestors through the śrāddha and worship;
to the sages, through celibacy, sacred learning, and asceticism. / What is offered, what is not offered, what is cast out, what is well-offered,
and what is tasted, O fortunate one—he should not omit the five sacrifices. / One should know that what is offered in the fire is huta; what is the rite of bali-offering is ahuta;
the offering of rice-balls, O Rāma, is called nirvāpya; / and prahuta is according to the sacrifice, as is the ancestral sacrifice;
and prāśana is likewise declared to be that which is eaten immediately afterwards. / A householder should always perform the five sacrifices for the removal of sin:
the sacrifice to the gods, the sacrifice to beings, and likewise the sacrifice to the ancestors, / the human sacrifice, and the sacrifice to the sages—he should perform these constantly, without sloth.

Thus ends the one hundred and third chapter, entitled 'Description of the Bath of Bṛhaspati', in the narrative of Puṣkara to Rāma, in the dialogue of Mārkaṇḍeya, in the second book of the auspicious Viṣṇudharmottara.
Rāma said: / Tell of another bath, O venerable one, which dispels misfortunes— / one which, performed just once, frees from sins.
Puṣkara said: / For those afflicted by consumption, those suffering from malarial fever, / those tormented by shadow-madness, those suffering from malarial fever,
those afflicted by the seizure of Vināyaka, and those whose minds are utterly deluded; / those whose learning does not succeed, whose crops do not grow,
whose family sinks into ruin, whose merchandise finds no sale; / the man who, even though strong, cannot win victory in battle;
those whose undertakings, though properly undertaken, fail again and again; / those who have quarrels with friends, kin, and relatives;
the woman whose fetus miscarries, or dies as soon as born; / whose menses waste away and no child is born;

The bath should be performed in autumn or spring for one who is healthy, O descendant of Bhṛgu; / but at all times for one who is afflicted, destroying planetary afflictions and diseases.
In that case, O twice-born, a brahmin should bathe [another brahmin] during the Sāvitra muhūrta; / he should bathe a kṣatriya during the Maitra and Vijaya muhūrtas;
he should bathe a vaiśya during the Paitāmaha muhūrta, and a śūdra during the remaining ones. / The fifth and seventh lunar days are best for the bathing of a brahmin;
the thirteenth and third are distinguished for a kṣatriya; / the second and tenth for a vaiśya, O giver of honor;
the sixth and fourteenth are for śūdras in the rite of bathing. / The eleventh, the twelfth, and the dark fourteenth
are celebrated for the rite of bathing of all castes. / Under the fixed asterisms, under the Vaiṣṇava asterism, Puṣya, Revatī, the asterism whose deity is Bhaga,
Hasta, Punarvasu, the Saumya asterism, Jyeṣṭhā, the Vāruṇa asterism, / and Dhaniṣṭhā—bathing is celebrated for all.

Attached [to residual karma], he undoubtedly goes from hell to an animal womb;
even there, O Bhārgava, he does not experience the sin in its entirety.
Then, with marks determined [by his acts], he experiences the remaining [karma] in human form.

Just as a rope bound with many strands has the power to pull heavy things,
so merit for heaven and sin for hell operate here for men, O foremost of the Bhṛgus.
2.114
Puṣkara said:
The soul having entered the womb remains in the embryonic mass;
stupified in that embryonic mass, it remains for just one month.
During the second month, it remains solid;
and the formation of its limbs occurs in the third month.
The epidermis and skin develop in the fifth month; in the sixth, the arising of body hairs occurs;
and likewise in the seventh month, its consciousness awakens.
Even as a frog afflicted by cold becomes dormant in the cold, so that soul,
situated within the womb, remains stupified for six months, O knower of dharma.
In the seventh month, it receives what is drunk and eaten by the mother;
then, in the eighth and ninth months, it suffers intense distress.
Enveloped by the caul, its hands are perpetually folded over its head;
a hermaphrodite lies in the middle, a female on the left, and a male on the right.

the second is blood-bearing, regarded as resting upon the liver and spleen; / the third is fat-bearing, which rests upon subtle and gross parts;
the fourth rests upon the marrow and is regarded as devoted to phlegm; / another is feces-bearing, by its residence in the colon;
the sixth is named bile-bearing, resting upon the digestive fire; / and the seventh is to be known as resting in the seminal receptacle, semen-bearing.
The five sense organs of intellect here are the ear, the nose, the eyes, / the skin, and the tongue; they have their foundation in the gross elements.
Likewise, the objects of the senses are five; hear their names from me: / sound is said to belong to the ear, [and] earthy smell belongs to the breath;
form is to be known of the eye, and touch belongs likewise to the skin; / and taste belongs to the tongue: they indeed have the gross elements as their support.
Here the organs of action are five; hear their names from me: / the anus and the genitals, the hands and the feet, and here the tongue is the fifth.

And from the waste product of blood arises that called the daṇḍaka; / and from the refined essence of fat and blood, the origin of the two kidneys is recorded.
From the refined essence of blood and flesh arise the intestines of embodied beings; / for women, one should designate them as numbering three and a half vyāmas;
people who know the Veda declare three vyāmas for women; / from the union of blood and wind is recorded the origin of the black [peritoneum].
From the refined essence of phlegm arises the heart resembling a lotus, / turned downward and hollow, in which the living being resides.
All the states accompanied by consciousness reside there; / on its left is the spleen, and on its right is the liver;
and on the right of that lotus, the kloman is proclaimed; / the channels that are in this body, carrying phlegm and blood,
from those, following the gross elements, the origin of the organs takes place. / The white circle of the two eyes comes from phlegm; the bilious [one comes from bile];

Sixteen tendons are declared in the body of embodied beings: / two are in each hand, and two are in each foot separately;
four are to be known as going to the back, and just as many in the neck. / Exactly sixteen are the nets here; their division is declared:
four each from flesh, sinews, vessels, and bones separately, / the wrists and ankles bound mutually;
and pegs are recorded here in the hands and in the feet, / and in the neck and the penis, declared by the wise.
And in this body are to be known four ropes of flesh; / two each are proclaimed there running on both sides of the backbone;
and just as many are these that bind them. / And seven are the sutures; five reside in the head,
one situated in the penis, and another situated in the tongue.
Here, the bones are three hundred and sixty,

including the fine ones: sixty-four teeth, twenty nails,
and the bone-pins of the hands and feet, which have four locations;
likewise, forty bones in the two shanks, and just as many [in the arms];
two each arising in the knees, cheeks, lips, shoulder blades, and shoulders;
eight in the eye-sockets; one should likewise assign [two] to the hip bones;
one pubic bone, and forty-five in the back;
fifteen bones of the neck, one collarbone, and likewise the jaw;
two at the root of the eyebrows, the forehead, eye[-sockets], cheeks, and nose-bones;
together with the ribs, sockets, and tumors, seventy-two;
two temples, four skull bones, and likewise the head;
the chest has seventeen bones: this is the enumeration of a person's bones.
And likewise, the joints here are two hundred and one hundred more;
sixty-eight in the limbs, sixty minus one [in the head and neck],
and eighty in the trunk are declared, O descendant of Bhṛgu;

nor are the ligaments considered here to be [only] two hundred, but thirty more;
and seventy ascending [ligaments] are declared in the trunk;
six [hundred] in the limbs have been declared by the wise.
Here there are five hundred muscles, and forty ascending;
four hundred here in the limbs [are taught] by the learned,
and sixty muscles in the trunk are declared by the wise;
and in women, thirty-four more should be known:
ten should be known in the breasts, and likewise ten in the vagina, O Rāma;
likewise four should be known in the uterus by students of gestation.
Thirty hundred thousand, and ten others indeed,
[and] fifty-six thousand vessels are declared;
they carry the nutrient fluid in the body, as channels do to a field,
[absorbing] anointing and everything else, such as sweat and ointments.
Know, O mighty-armed one, that there are seventy-two crores of body hairs;

of marrow, of fat, and of tallow, O twice-born,
of urine, and of bile, phlegm, and feces,
and of blood and nutrient fluid, the handfuls are remembered in order:
each is remembered as half a handful greater than the preceding half-handful;
half a handful of semen, and likewise half of vital essence;
and of the menstrual blood of women, four are declared by the wise.
This measure is stated by students of the body for one of balanced elements.
The bodies of embodied beings are always varied;
they differ according to their differentiation, conforming to wastes and humoral faults.
This body is a lump of wastes and humoral faults, the resting-place of the elements, bound under the sway of karman;
fortunate is he for whom in this world it leads to liberation, and the supreme cause of liberation is indeed Viṣṇu.

experiencing pain there, he does not die, O giver of honor.
That path is declared to be exceedingly rough and dreadful to behold;
assuming all forms, O Rāma, covered with cold on earth, vast,
abounding in falls of snow, extremely cold, and exceedingly difficult to traverse;

And those who inflict the torments of the hells upon men / are declared to have terrible forms, inspiring terror at mere sight.
Some have the faces of cats, owls, jackals, vultures, hawks, and donkeys; / others, of elephants, horses, camels, boars, and lions;
and of monitor lizards, buffaloes, crows, madgu cormorants, and crane birds; / of rams, goats, moths, serpents, and mice;
and of other creatures too; likewise, others are pot-bellied men; / others have faces on their shoulders, appearing headless like a trunk;
having terrible faces, terrifying, bearing various weapons, / of harsh speech, unassailable, entwined with serpents and scorpions.
Throwing men into a vat of oil, kindling a fire / that is well blazing, they boil some men again and again.
And others on frying pans, and others in copper vessels, / and others in iron vessels, and even in molten sparks of fire;
having impaled others on stakes, and others in dry fire, / cutting them open, they thoroughly cook them, and likewise others after cutting them up.

for seven nights from three teats; for seven nights from two teats;
likewise for six nights from one teat; and for three nights one should subsist on air.
This vow is named somāyana, the destroyer of sins.
For three days one should drink warm water; for three days one should drink warm milk;

Āyuṣya, Svastyayana, the entire Varmagaṇa, / Vāstoṣpatya, and Raudra—these are declared to be the ten groups.
An oblation of these is to be understood in all rites of pacification, O Bhārgava. / The first three rituals are proclaimed according to distinct principal categories.
For each group, he should give a cow; a cow for the interior deposit, and a cow for the amulet. / A ruler of the earth, however, should give ten for each.
Free from desire, he should also give land to the royal priest. / For Abhayā, an amulet of Varuṇa's [tree] should be made, O foremost of the Bhṛgus;
an amulet of hundred-jointed grass for Amṛtā; and for Saumyā, an amulet made from a conch. / For Vaiṣṇavī, an amulet of trivṛt is declared; for Raudrī, a washed amulet;
for Brāhmī, an amulet of brahmarakṣas; for Maitrī, it should be of gold. / For Vāyavyā, an amulet of aṅgiras is declared, and Vāyavya should be made of conch.
For Kauberī, of udumbara; for Bhārgavī, of asparagus; / for Prājāpatyā, a bilva amulet; for Tvāṣṭrī, a consecrated amulet;

It comes to fruition in the king's body, in the populace, in his city, wives, domestic priest, / in his sons, and likewise in his treasury and army.
Those portents which occur naturally at their assigned times / are to be known as bringing good fortune; hear about them as I speak.
Lightning, thunderbolts, earthquakes, twilight glows, sounds of windless air, / halos, dust, smoke, and a red sun at sunset and sunrise;
the blessing of trees here, flowing with abundant flowers and fruits, / and an increase in cattle, birds, and honey—these are auspicious in the spring months of Madhu and Mādhava.
A solar disk turbid with the falling of stars and meteors, and tawny, / bringing wind amidst fireless blazes, bursts, smoke, and dust;
the twilight red as a red lotus, the sky resembling an agitated ocean, / and the drying up of the water of rivers—having seen [these] in summer, one should declare it auspicious.
Indra's bow, halos, the moon, dry sprouts shooting up, / shaking, overflowing, deformity, and a dread trembling of the earth;
the swelling of rivers, wells, and lakes, the swimming of mules, / and the crumbling of houses by day—these will occur in the rainy season [as auspicious].

And worship of the denizens of heaven should indeed be performed with the sounds of mṛdaṅgas, drums, and with songs; / likewise, sacrifice to Dhātṛ should be performed according to rule, and food with many cows should be given to twice-borns.
2.142 / Garga said: / If unyoked conveyances move, or yoked ones do not move / when urged, great misfortune would be impending for him.
Musical instruments being played do not sound, or they sound unstruck; / immovable things move, and movable things do not move;
and sounds of trumpets in the sky, or sounds of celestial musicians moving [there]; / if wood turns into an axe or a transformation of a wooden ladle;
if the tail of cows entangles and fells a woman; / or eclipses caused by celestial portents—one should predict dreadful peril from weapons.
Having performed the worship of Vāyu with barley groats, O twice-born, one should recite the mantras prescribed for him; / one should give abundant supreme food here, ending with circumambulation, so that his pacification may occur.

And cows accompanied by gold, a pair of cloths, and upper garments should be given to twice-borns according to rule; / when this is done, the evil that was heralded by wild beasts or birds goes to pacification.
2.144 / Garga said: / The causeless collapse of firm temples, arched gateways, watchtowers, / gates, walls, and dwellings leads to the death of the king.
Where the quarters of the sky are covered with dust or smoke,
and the sun, moon, and stars are discoloured, it leads to an increase of fear.
Where rākṣasas are seen, and brahmins act contrary to dharma,
the seasons are reversed, and the unworthy of worship is worshipped by people;
the asterisms are disjoined—that is a token of great danger;
and the rising of a comet, eclipses, and the piercing of the sun and moon;
where there is an aberration of the planets and asterisms, there too one should predict fear;
and women quarrel, and children strike children;
and the twice-born are bereft of character and proper conduct, fond of liquor, meat, and untruth,
as well as excessively rigid as stone, fallen from the path of the three Vedas;

in whatever country [such things] arise, there one should recognise calamity;
where [gods] are not continually worshipped with the proper sacrificial posts, food, and oblations;
where there occurs an interruption of proper ritual acts;
where the fire does not blaze up when appeasing oblations are poured;
ants and carnivorous [creatures] go to the afflicted person;
full jars leak, or the sacrificial offering is stolen;
where auspicious voices are not heard on every side;
a sneezing person runs, and [people] laugh loudly and weep;
and they do not conduct themselves properly toward the gods and toward brahmins;
and secret faults are voiced, and discordant [instruments] are played;
where people harm their preceptors and friends, and are intent on honouring enemies;
where the populace dishonours brahmins, friends, and ministers;
where atheism arises concerning rites of appeasement, auspicious rites, and oblations into the fire;
there the king dies, or that realm is destroyed.
When the destruction of a king is imminent, hear from me the portents:
first he hates brahmins, and comes into conflict with brahmins;

he is also reviled by brahmins, and seeks to kill brahmins;
he does not remember them in duties, and being petitioned, he is overcome;
he takes delight [in wickedness], is merciless, and does not welcome their praise;
and through greed he imposes unprecedented taxes on the people.

Puṣkara said:
One's own circle [of kings] indeed should be the first circle here;
around it are to be understood the enemies of the circle.
Hear the arrangement regarding the target of attack there as well:
the enemy is deemed the target of attack, and the expectant ally there [is also to be known];
beyond him is to be known the friend, the ally, and the ally's enemy;
this has been told in the front; learn from me also regarding what lies to the rear:
the rear-enemy is behind him, then the rear-ally is spoken of;
another beyond that is the rear-enemy's ally, then the rear-ally's ally is spoken of.
Capable of punishing or favouring an ambitious king who has taken up arms
or laid down arms, O twice-born, is proclaimed the middle king;
he who is capable of punishing or favouring all [others]
is called the neutral king, a mighty ruler of the earth.
Just this circle of twelve kings has been declared to you, O Rāma;
even here no fixed rule can be stated, O bull among men.

There is truly no enemy by birth, nor is there any natural friend;
friends and enemies are produced through the connection of utility.
Enemies are declared to be of three kinds: born of the same family, immediate neighbours, and made;
among these, each preceding one is considered weightier and hardest to remedy.
Even an immediate neighbour who is an enemy is also considered by me as an artificial [enemy];
a king may become a rear-enemy to an enemy who attacks a friend.
One should pacify one's own rear-enemy through diplomatic expedients;
the ancients do not praise the destruction of an enemy by means of an ally.
For an ally becomes an enemy immediately on becoming a neighbour;
the ambitious king should uproot the enemy himself if he is able.

The ambitious king who conquers righteously should thus bring the world into subjugation;
people are terrified of him who conquers unrighteously;
even after obtaining the whole earth, he does not enjoy prosperity for long.

Rāma said:
Conciliation and division have been described, and likewise gift and force;
force has been described within one's own land; describe [its application] in a foreign land to me.

Entering into an agreement is considered peace, while injury is war;
the marching of one desirous of conquest into the enemy's realm is prescribed as an expedition.
Remaining in one's own country even during hostilities is called encamping;
advancing with half of one's forces is called duplicity.
Seeking refuge with a neutral or intermediary power is known as alliance;
peace should be sought with an equal, and by an inferior with one more powerful.
War should be made personally by a stronger king against an inferior;
even then, a rear-enemy who is stronger should not be allied with.
When an enemy, holding back, is capable of interrupting undertakings,
a powerful king whose rear is insecure should resort to duplicity.
A king who considers himself checked by a powerful foe
should seek refuge; this is the lowest of the policy measures.
Marching is declared to involve great loss, expense, and fatigue for them;
only if it yields great advantage should it be undertaken, O Rāma.

You are the highest earth together with what roams the mountains;
likewise the sky together with the sun and darkness;
together with the ocean, together with the timiṅgila fish, together with waves, having many sea-monsters, thronged with fish.

You are constantly worshipped with sacrifices here by brahmins for the sake of fruit;
O host of forces, you are sung in the eight distinctions of purpose;
for your sake, the foremost of the twice-born, devoted to sacrifice, master the auxiliary limbs of the Veda together with all the Vedas.

Upon seeing a second adverse omen, however, he should re-enter his house.
Now I shall speak of the desired, auspicious omens, O sinless one:
White flowers, the foremost ones, and likewise a full water-jar,
aquatic beings, fish, meat, and fish, O descendant of Bhṛgu;
cows, horses, elephants, and a single tethered animal;
gods, friends, brahmins, and a blazing fire;
fortunate courtesans, cow dung blessed by ancient teachers,
gold, silver, copper, and likewise all kinds of gems;
medicinal herbs, O omniscient one, as well as sweet flag and white mustard;
a palanquin carried by men, and likewise an auspicious seat;
a sword, an umbrella, a banner, earth, and weapons;
all the insignia of royalty, and a corpse without weeping;
ghee, curds, milk, and various kinds of fruits;
a svastika, a vardhamāna, a nandyāvarta, together with the Kaustubha gem;

rivers depicted in paintings, and other auspicious things;
and principal unbroken grains of rice, and a mirror;
collyrium, yellow orpiment, ornament, and honey;
a conch shell, sugarcane, sweetmeats, and pleasant utterances;
the deep and charming opening sound of musical instruments;
and the notes gāndhāra, ṣaḍja, and ṛṣabha are recommended on a journey.
A wind full of gravel, harsh, blowing from the cardinal points,
adverse, and blowing low is to be known as fear-causing, O twice-born.
Favorable, gentle, smooth, and pleasant to the touch, it brings happiness.
Bears, harsh-sounding creatures, rhinoceroses, and carnivorous beasts are condemned.
Dense, glossy clouds resembling the trumpeting of elephants are auspicious;
a lightning flash moving favorably is praised, and likewise the rainbow of Śakra.
Torrents of yellow rain should be known as inauspicious;
planets moving favorably are praised, especially the lord of the direction.

At the time of birth, from the place where the Sun is situated, in the third, sixth, tenth, and eleventh; from Mars's place, in the first, second, fourth, seventh, eighth, ninth, tenth, and eleventh; from Mercury, in the third, fifth, sixth, ninth, tenth, eleventh, and twelfth; from Jupiter, in the fifth, sixth, ninth, and eleventh; from Venus, in the sixth, seventh, and twelfth; from Saturn, in the first, second, fourth, seventh, eighth, ninth, tenth, and eleventh; from the ascendant, in the third, fourth, sixth, tenth, eleventh, and twelfth; [for the Moon] from the Sun's place, in the third, seventh, eighth, tenth, and eleventh; from its own, in the first, third, sixth, seventh, tenth, and eleventh; from Venus, in the third, fourth, fifth, seventh, ninth, and eleventh; for Mars, from the Sun, in the third, fifth, sixth, tenth, and eleventh; from its own, in the first, second, fourth, seventh, eighth, tenth, and eleventh; from Mercury, in the third, fifth, sixth, tenth, eleventh, and twelfth; from Venus, in the sixth, eighth, eleventh, and twelfth; from Saturn, in the first, fourth, seventh, eighth, tenth, and eleventh; from the ascendant, in the first, third, sixth, tenth, and eleventh; for Mercury, from the Sun, in the fifth, sixth, eighth, ninth, and eleventh; from the Moon, in the second, fourth, sixth, eighth, tenth, and eleventh; from Mars, in the first, second, fourth, seventh, eighth, ninth, and eleventh; from its own, in the first, third, fifth, sixth, ninth, eleventh, and twelfth, [it is] auspicious;
from Jupiter, in the sixth, eighth, eleventh, and twelfth, [it is] auspicious;
from Venus, in the first, third, fourth, fifth, eighth, ninth, and eleventh; from Saturn, in the first, second, fourth, seventh, eighth, ninth, tenth, and eleventh; from the ascendant, in the first, second, fourth, sixth, eighth, tenth, and eleventh. Jupiter [is auspicious] from the Sun in the first, second, third, fourth, seventh, eighth, tenth, and eleventh; from the ninth (dharma), in the second, fifth, seventh, ninth, and eleventh; from Mars, in the first, second, third, fourth, seventh, eighth, tenth, and eleventh; from Mercury, in the first, second, fourth, fifth, sixth, seventh, tenth, and eleventh; from Jupiter, in the first, second, third, fourth, seventh, eighth, tenth, and eleventh;

Standing at the beginning, he should practice hitting the target situated square;
immediately after that, [the target that is] sharp, and that which is situated turning away.
Then he should practice the raised target at the end of the elevated mark, [and] the tuṣyaka;
then, with the bow placed in these target positions,
and with relaxed [fingers] placed on the arm-guard, he should shoot even with the hands [themselves].
In that target, O Rāma, two targets are known as 'firm';
know two targets to be 'puṣkara', and likewise two to be 'citrapuṣkara'.
The square and the sharp are declared to be the firm targets;
the square, the broad-sharp […].
The low [target] is designated as puṣkara, as is the target that is situated high.
The lower targets are in the puṣkara, while [the other two] are designated as citrapuṣkara.
Having thus made a group of targets on the right and on the other side,
the steadfast man, having conquered the target, should then first mount [a vehicle].
This very rule has been declared, seen by practitioners there;

a man, taking a club in his hand from the right thumb,
raising it, should strike firmly at the head of that named [target];
he should cause its downfall with both hands.

This model, accompanied by rule, is beneficial and a true release for pupils, provided by me whose mind seeks your welfare;
for the sake of enduring the fatigue of battle for mounts, it is to be struck down first by the wise man as worthy of training.
Puṣkara said:
Whirling, whirling upward, tossed, leaped, issued forth, sped,
fallen, raised up, and unagitated army-fall;
striking upward, holding down, and left and right,
unseen, sun-burst, terrible, and kingly wide-mouthed;
deformed, falling away, terrifying, fearful,
complete or third, foot-by-foot, lotus-born, and enemy-born;
pratyālīḍha, anālīḍha, and boar's wallow—
thus thirty-two sword-movements for holding a shield are to be known.
Straight, extended, broad, and bent sideways:
five movements of the dart in single combat have been indicated by the great-souled ones.
Severing, threatening, dropping, revolving, and whirling, / as well as slicing, cutting, and wheel-whirling;

Both [kinds] are to be known by the nature of prose and verse, endowed with their characteristics;
and verse there is of two kinds: regular metre and free-formed metre.
There are twenty-one nominal case-endings; a word is held to end in either nominal or verbal endings.
The first person is one, and the second is considered the middle [person];
and the third is called the highest: this triad expresses their action.
There are nine verbal terminations called active voice,

The nominal stem is one's own base here, and that base they also wish to be the verbal root;
by this all literature is composed, whether prose or together with verse.
Mārkaṇḍeya said:
With a quarter of six syllables, the metre is called Gāyatrī;
Uṣṇih has seven syllables, and Anuṣṭubh has eight syllables;
Bṛhatī has nine syllables, and that designated Paṅkti has ten syllables;
Triṣṭubh has eleven syllables, and Jagatī has twelve syllables.
That with thirteen syllables is to be known with the prefix 'ati-' likewise;
and Śakvarī has fourteen syllables, O delight of the Yadus.
Atiśakvarī is declared to have fifteen syllables;
they recognise the sixteen-syllabled metre as that which is recorded as Aṣṭi.
The metre named Atyaṣṭi has seventeen syllables;
and that with eighteen syllables is proclaimed as named Dhṛti.
Likewise, that named Atidhṛti has twenty minus one;
and twenty syllables make that named Kṛti, O best of the Yadus.

Twenty joined with one is declared to be Prakṛti;
and twenty-two is likewise declared to be named Ākṛti.
Twenty-three is Vikṛti, and that with one [more] is considered Saṃkṛti;
and Saṃkṛti joined with one is declared to be Atikṛti, O king.
And Utkṛti of twenty-six [syllables] is the metre spoken of by the wise.
One should designate these respectively by their four quarters.
A light syllable is of one measure, marked with a clear stroke;
it should be placed so, while a heavy [syllable] has two measures and should be curved.
Even a light syllable which is final becomes heavy, O king;
and long and protracted [syllables] are likewise called heavy.
A light syllable before which conjunct consonants follow is considered heavy.
By this distinction of light and heavy syllables, the permutation [is formed], O Yādava.
All the varieties of metres arise in many ways, O king;
I shall explain how they are generated, listen to that:

Being distinct and varied in region after region, it cannot be fully stated through grammatical rules; / that which is called corrupt (apabhraṣṭa) in the worlds is to be understood as the domain of one who knows the region.
3.8
Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Devas, divaukasas, divyas, gīrvāṇas, tridaśas, suras [are synonyms for the gods]; / Hari, Janārdana, Viṣṇu, Vāsudeva, and Adhokṣaja;
Nārāyaṇa, Hṛṣīkeśa, the wielder of the discus, he whose mount is Garuḍa; / Bala, Saṃkarṣaṇa, Ananta, Śeṣa, he who has the palmyra on his banner, the plowman;
Pradyumna, Kāmadeva, the archer, he whose banner bears the makara; / Aniruddha whose weapon is the sword, and likewise he whose banner bears the ṛṣya deer;
Lakṣmī, Karīṣiṇī, Śrī, and also the goddess Vibhāvarī; / the discus is called Sudarśana, and the bow of Viṣṇu is Śārṅga;
The bow of Śiva is named Pināka; that belonging to Varuṇa is Gāṇḍīva; / the bow of Śakra is declared to be Ājagava; the vajra is called the kuliśa;
The mace of Viṣṇu is declared to be Kaumodakī, O king; / the plow is called Lalāma, and the pestle is remembered as Sānanda;

Pāñcajanya is remembered as the conch which Janārdana bears; / the lotus in his hand is mentioned, and likewise that called Astrālokya;
Bhūmi, Vasundharā, Pṛthvī, Kṣamā, Kṣoṇī, Dharā, Mahī [for the earth]; / nabhas, kha, gagana, and likewise ākāśa, the stride of Hari [for the sky];
Tārkṣya, Suparṇa, Garuḍa, the mount and emblem on the banner of Hari; / the serpent Ananta, the bearer of the plow, Śeṣa, he whose banner bears the palmyra, the plowman;
Brahmā, the Grandfather, Śambhu, the Self-born, Creator of Beings; / Mahādeva, Bhava, Śambhu, Tryambaka, the husband of Pārvatī;
Umā, Satī, she who was born of the mountain, and likewise she who was born of Menakā; / Bhadrakālī is remembered as Durgā, and the gaṇas are remembered as the Pramathas;

matallīskhalita, bhramara, pārśvasvastika, / madādvilasita, vikṣipta, gatimaṇḍala;
vaiśākharecita, parivṛttakarecita, / udvartaka, paricchinna, recaka, then balāhaka;
sambhrānta, vāṅgika, krīḍa, alātaka, / vidyudbhrānta, parāvṛtta, together with pārśvaccheda;
ākṣipta, recita, saumya, then karita, / sūcīviddha, apaviddha, vilāpa, and avanikuṭṭita.
These thirty-six are declared as the aṅgahāras, O best of the Yadus. / I shall explain their basic units in due order, / and how the movement of hands and feet is to be applied by the practitioners.
The basic unit is the production of all the aṅgahāras; / I shall explain them by their names and by their movements.
A karaṇa of dance is combined with [specific movements of] hands and feet; / two dance-karaṇas constitute a mātṛkā, O king.

Lalita, daṇḍapakṣa, bhujaṅgatrastarecita, / nūpura, recita, bhramara, and catura;
bhujaṅgāñcita is another, chinna, vṛścikarecita, / latāvṛścika is another, chinna, vṛścikarecita;
vṛścika, vyaṃsita, and sūcīviddhaka, / lalāṭatilaka, kuṇḍala, cakramaṇḍala;
tarormaṇḍala, ākṣipta, latālasita, argala, / vikṣipta, bhramita, vilāsa, and vānarapluta;
parivṛtta, nivṛtta, pārśvākrānta, nikuñcita, / atikrānta, avakrānta, dolāpādavivarjita;
nāgaprakrīḍita, vipluta, garuḍapluta, / gaṇḍasūcī, parikṣipta, and pārśvajānuka;
gṛhāvalīnaka, līna, viṣṇukrānta, ajapluta, / ākrānta, maṇḍita, mayūralasita, and nasa;
sarpita, colapāda, and hariṇapluta, / preṅkhālita, nitamba, skhalita, and parihastaka;

In holding a branch, gathering clusters of blossoms, sitting with rolling eyes and lamenting, / and in natural conversation, this posture should be assumed, O foremost of the Yādava lineage.
3.24 / Mārkaṇḍeya said: / Hear from me as I first explain the movement of the head. / When, from its natural straight [position], it is tossed upward once suddenly,
it should be used in gesturing, instructing, questioning, and in natural conversation. / When it is moved repeatedly, it is regarded as kampita.
It is seen in anger, threatening, argumentation, and solemn declarations. / Moving the head slowly to one side is regarded as dhuta;
it is used in despondency, wonder, and prohibition. / Shaking [the head] from side to side is named vidhuta;
it occurs in one afflicted with cold or suffering from fever, in an intoxicated person, and in one who is hurried. / In commencing a circular movement, it is reproached.
It is desired in accomplishment, wonder, joy, and playfulness. / When [the head] is raised upward once, it is regarded as udvāhita;

Thus the action of the head has been described to you, O king of men. / Añcita, recita, mukta, vivṛta, and likewise catura,
prasārita, and likewise stabdha, should be indicated in the movements of the neck. / Añcita is slightly to one shoulder, but from rolling it is recita;
recita is from turning around, mukta [is released], and vivṛta is moved; / catura is declared sweet, and prasārita is extended;
and stabdha is declared rigid, and it follows the movement of the head. / Añcita should be in playful grace and the like, recita in glancing around in a circle;
mukta [should be used] in states of fainting and the like, vivṛtta in graceful movements and the like; / catura in a gentle state, and prasārita should be done in gazing;
stabdha in cold or disease, naturally motionless. / Vibuddha, vinivṛtta, nirbhugna, and also bhugna,
and likewise vivṛtta and ṛju should be indicated in its action. / Turned away is named vibuddha; nivṛtta is covered;

facing downward is avābhugna; turned upward is nibhugna; / bhugna is slightly bent; nivṛtta is opened;
and ṛju rests in its natural state: [now] their actions are spoken of. / It should be performed in warding off what is wrong, in phrases such as 'Not so!',
vinivṛtta in indignation, and in actions born of jealousy and anger; / vibhugna is to be known in profound gazing and the like;
bhugna should be employed in shame, and naturally for ascetics; / nivṛtta also should be performed in laughter, sorrow, fear, and the like;
it should be performed in the natural upright position, O delight of the Yadus. / Ābhugna, then nirbhugna, and likewise prakampita,

With the upper eyelid fallen, tearful, and with pupils lost [from sight], it is the pathetic [gaze]; / with the tips of the eyelashes slightly contracted, and the pupils slightly turned up,
even and wide open, that is held to be the marvellous gaze. / Raised and with crooked knitting of the brows, the furious is described;
with dilated centre and even pupils, the heroic is described. / With the pupils cast toward the outer corners, the odious gaze is described;
unblinking through meditation, moving downward, [such] is the peaceful. / With wide-open centre, sweet, loving, and gently smiling is considered [the loving gaze];
with dilated centre, quivering, joyful, looking toward the periphery [is the joyful gaze]. / With crooked pupils directed downward, concealed, submerged in looking [is the deceitful gaze];
with crooked knitting of the brows, and motionless pupils, is the angry [gaze]. / Contracted and with motionless pupils is the bashful [gaze];
greatly desired by those of noble spirit, wide open, is the proud [gaze]. / Even, with the outer corners expanded, is said to be the astonished [gaze];
even and with even pupils, is declared to be the gentle [gaze]. / With contracted eyelids, rough, and with averted pupils, is the frightened [gaze];

one whose two eyelids quiver is called the dejected [gaze]. / The gaze which has contracted eyelids is the squinting [gaze];
with slightly raised eyelids, that is prescribed as the tranquil [gaze]. / With slow pupils and eyelids, it is the distressed and faint [gaze];
that which is drooping and quivering is called the disturbed [gaze]. / With pupils slightly stiffened, it is called the despondent gaze;
and with unsteady pupils, it is celebrated as the apprehensive [gaze]. / Harsh, with playful pupils, and displaying fear, it is the frightened [gaze];
wide open and with unblinking eyes, it is named the sorrowless [gaze]. / With unsteady eyes and wandering pupils, it is the bewildered [gaze];
with slightly curved pupils, it is the contracted gaze. / With stationary pupils and eyelids, looking evenly, is the vacant [gaze] in agitation;
with trembling tips of the eyelashes, bud-like, is the released [gaze]. / With turned-up eyelids and eyelashes, and rigid pupils, is the reflective [gaze];
wide open and with rolling pupils, it is named the intoxicated [gaze]. / With blossoming corners, charming, and smiling, it is regarded as the graceful [gaze];

with drooping eyelashes, tearful at the tip, and forming a bud, it is called the languid [gaze]. / The lovely gaze is in sidelong glances, but the terrible is in slaying;
the comic is to be known in laughter, and the pathetic in the pathetic sentiment. / In the marvellous, the marvellous [gaze] should be used, and the furious is prescribed in the furious [sentiment];
the heroic should be employed in the heroic [sentiment], and the odious in disgust. / The peaceful is prescribed in the peaceful [sentiment], and the loving in an affectionate glance;
the joyful should be used in delight, the oblique should be used in malice. / The angry is prescribed in rage, and the frightened should likewise be in fear;
the bashful is called for in shame, and the proud in states of energy. / The astonished gaze is in wonder, and the gentle in a gentle glance;
the dejected should be used in separation, and the squinting in love's union. / The weary should be used for one who is exhausted, but the distressed in despair;
the disturbed is in misery, and the despondent in dejection. / The apprehensive is in suspicion, and the frightened is prescribed in terror;

Where the two eyelids are brought together, that is closed; / where the eyelids have their bases dropped, that is called beaten.
In unpleasant smell and touch, contracting should be known by the wise. / For the purpose of wonder and in the heroic, that [elevation] should be performed and perfected;
in the erotic [sentiment], the level should be used, and in a blow, the beaten. / In women's lovers' quarrels, throbbing is desired here;
in cold, wind, heat, and rain, the closed should indeed be performed. / In anger, rolling should be used, and the level in closing and opening.
Turning, revolving, moving, disappearing, / rolling, splitting, and also astonishment,
turning upward, and natural: these are held to be the actions of the pupils. / Circular motion between the eyelids is known as revolving;
turning is sideways movement; from turning comes moving. / Drawing inward is entering, from going within;

and from moving to the outer corner, it is called elated. / Dropping is the condition of sinking down, and issuing forth from going outward;
turning back is from moving upward, and natural is from one's own nature. / In laughter, turning should be used, and revolving in intoxication;
in the heroic sentiment there should be moving, and in the odious, entering; / rolling is in the erotic, and dropping in the pathetic sentiment;
issuing forth is in the terrible, and turning upward in the heroic. / In the remaining states, one should employ the natural as appropriate.
There is the straight, and the sidelong; after this, the following; / looking, looking upward, and looking downward,
with level pupils and gentle: that is the straight eye-movement. / With pupils gone inside the eyelashes: that is called sidelong here;
employed in delineating forms, it is known as following. / What is seeing suddenly is held to be looking;
looking upward is known as gazing up, and looking on all sides [is surveying]. / Looking to the two sides is viewing sideways, and looking behind is looking downward.

And one should employ the action of the eyebrows following the glances; / for indeed the eye glides along, resting on various emotions and rasas.
The movement of the eyebrows should indeed occur in accordance with the eye: / raising, falling, knitting the brow, and charming;
contracted, curled, and also natural. / Raising the eyebrows is utkṣepa (raising); lowering is patana (falling).
From raising the root of both [eyebrows] upward, bhrukuṭī (knitting the brow) is declared. / Catura (charming) is from a slight elevation of a sweet, elongated eyebrow.
Of one or both there may be udbhagna (broken) or trikuñcita (triply curved); / from the graceful movement of one, it is proclaimed by name as recita (emptied).
Learn from me the function of the eyebrows in their proper place and natural state: / raising [is used] in amorous sport, falling from compassion;
and the knitting of the brow is to be known in anger, and catura in the erotic [sentiment]. / [The cheek] is to be known as kṣāma (wasted) and thin, phulla (blossoming) is expanded;

sampūrṇa (full) is called filled out, kampita (trembling) is shaken; / śṛṅgabhinna (pointed/furrowed) is contracted, and saromāñca (horripilating) is of such kind;
the natural state is called prākṛta (ordinary) and manda (calm) by the wise. / Wasted should be employed in grief, phulla in joy;
full should be made in satisfaction, and trembling in rage; / contracted is prescribed in laughter, and horripilating in touch;
and the ordinary, resting in its natural state, one should employ according to the connection of meaning. / Bent, sluggish, drawn, panting, wrinkled, turned down—
and in its natural state should the nose be executed in performance by the wise. / Turned down is to be known as natā, and sluggish has drooping nostrils;
drawn is contracted and raised upward, panting is filled with breath; / from contracting it is kūṇitā (wrinkled), and one's nose resting in its normal condition is svabhāvasthā (natural).
The turned-down [nose] should be used in what is pleasing, sluggish is prescribed in grief; / drawn in strong fragrance, and panting in beloved [encounters];

Having cupped the hands together, one should direct them to the left nostril;
curling the middle finger on the right, one should likewise bend both thumbs.
Then, having formed the shape of a trident, one should turn it towards the heart;
this is remembered as the mudrā of Aghora, destroying all obstacles.

and one hundred is stated for the Rucaka, and ten less [than a hundred] for the Śaśaka.
A width of twelve aṅgulas is called a tāla.
The height of the foot from the ankle is proclaimed to be four aṅgulas;
and the two shanks are two tālas, and the two knees are equal to the foot.
And the two thighs are equal to the shanks; the navel from the penis is one tāla;
and from the navel to the heart is the same, and from the heart to the throat;
the neck should be a third of a tāla, and the face should be a tāla;
and the crown above the forehead is stated to be a sixth of a tāla.
The penis should be known to be in the middle: thus is length proclaimed.
The hand is declared to be a tāla, O king; the upper arms are seventeen aṅgulas;
the forearms are declared to be just as much; half of the chest is eight [aṅgulas].
This is declared as the measure in length of the Haṃsa, O king.
According to this same method one should calculate for the others as well;
in length and circumference they are all symmetrical, O ruler of men.

An excessive one produces sorrow, and a wretched one [does the same], O best of kings; / a dry one leads to loss of wealth, and an angry one magnifies fear.
An image with an emaciated belly should not be made, nor one with an overly protruding belly; / nor should one with a wound be fashioned, O descendant of Yadu;
nor one deficient or excessive in proportions, nor of a dry complexion, / nor with an open mouth, nor bent, O delighter of the Yadus.

A person lacking proper proportion might suffer on earth by the force of time and emotion; / reflecting thus with his own intellect, the wise man should determine proportion in the application of diminution and increase.
Mārkaṇḍeya said: / Having mixed brick powder of three kinds with a third part of clay, / [one should add] bdellium resin, along with beeswax, licorice, muruka, and molasses;
having combined safflower with oil, one should add an equal part [of it]; / one should pulverise there a third part of lime burnt by fire;
having mixed that with two parts of bilva fruit, and adding an ink-like astringent, / the intelligent man should then add a portion of sand according to requirement;
then he should steep it with slimy bark-decoction water, / and leave that entirely moistened [mixture] to stand for just a month.
Having carefully taken it out when it has attained softness after a month, / the skilled man should apply the plaster on the wall, inspecting it once dry,
smooth, even, well-adherent, free from depressions and elevations, / neither too thick nor too thin.

having drawn [the outline], the wise [painter] should establish [it] in proportion and in posture;
then he should colour it with pigments according to the requirements of the place.
Whether his complexion is dark or fair, he should display that [complexion];
and its characteristic has been stated at length, O king, when it arises.
Five primary colours are recorded: white, yellow, in reverse order,
black, and blue, O best of kings; hundreds are known through mixtures.
By the division of the primary colours and through the conception of the mood,
he should produce colours by the hundreds and thousands through his own intellect.
A prominent mixture among blues is praised as 'pālāśa' [leaf-green];
it is pure, mixed with white, and also [one] where blue predominates.
And one [colour] exceeds [another] in accordance with the complexions;
whether white predominates, is lesser, or [they are] in equal parts, it is thus threefold.
Combined with a single binding medium, it is thus differentiated in manifold ways;
by it, dūrvā-sprout, yellow, and auspicious kapittha-green,

The wise man should have Brahmā made: gentle, four-faced, / seated in the lotus posture, pleasing, and clad in a black-antelope skin;
wearing matted locks, four-armed, standing in a chariot with seven swans; / one pair of his arms should rest in the palm of his left hand;
in one right hand, an auspicious rosary, / and a water-pot in the second; wearing all ornaments;
endowed with all auspicious marks, of peaceful form, O king; / lustrous as the tip of a lotus petal, with eyes closed in meditation.
One should fashion the god in an idol, or in a painting, or in modeling. / And one should make Viṣṇu, the god of gods, seated on Garuḍa.
His chest illuminated by the Kaustubha jewel, wearing all kinds of ornaments, / having the hue of a water-laden cloud, and wearing divine yellow garments; 3.44.10
having four faces, four bodies, and twice as many arms: / the eastern face should be gentle, while the southern face is Narasiṃha; 11

Then, on that portion of the auspicious pedestal, one should place a lotus
having eight auspicious petals; in its middle, on the pericarp, the sun.
There on the petals, one should arrange the guardians of the directions according to their directions;
beneath the auspicious pedestal one should fashion the earth.
They know the pillar to be the atmosphere, and the upper part beyond that;
all the foremost gods are present there.
The sky, consisting of all the gods, has been described to you, O strong-armed one;
having worshipped it, one obtains all desires.
This essential nature of the sky has been described by me; by performing worship of the sky, the entire
universe, moving and unmoving, would be worshipped, O sole hero, O foremost of the Yādava lineage!

Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Nara should be made dark like dūrvā grass and two-armed, O strong-armed one;
Nārāyaṇa has four arms and the radiance of a blue lotus petal.

resplendent with a small circle placed within an even crescent moon. / The storeys which I have stated, and likewise the spotless star-shaped [stone]—
they are to be known by you, O king, as the fourteen worlds. / In the liṅga is the god Maheśvara; the round pillar is the Grandfather [Brahmā];
and the pillar which is square is the god Janārdana. / Likewise, the three auspicious pedestals are to be understood as the nature of the three qualities [guṇas];
they are said to be the receptacle of the qualities, the triple world with the moving and unmoving. / Beneath the worlds, and above the liṅga, O king,
the guardians of the world should be made, with tridents in their hands, in the four directions: / Virūḍha, Dhṛtarāṣṭra, and Virūpākṣa, O Yādava,
and Kubera of great splendour, auspicious, wearing the attire of Sūrya; / all should be made clad in armour and adorned with auspicious ornaments.
Know Virūḍhaka [to be] the god Śakra, the lord of the Gaṇas; / know Dhṛtarāṣṭra [to be] Yama, the ruler of the world;
know Virūpākṣa [to be] Varuṇa, the lord of aquatic creatures; / know the king of kings Kubera [to be] the lord who bestows wealth.

it should be furnished with diverse figures, neither round nor trident-like. / But eight spires should be made there provided with niches;
adorned with niches accompanied by latticed windows, O king. / Everywhere the spires should be made with auspicious āmalaka finials,
provided with wheels, pennants, and banners, O ruler of men. / Raised high, this temple shines as though clinging to the sky.
Learn from me the placement of deities in this temple in proper sequence: / Vāsudeva should be made here facing the eastern direction, O king,
or facing the western direction, but not facing any other quarter. / And the direction of Vāsudeva here should be considered the east, O king;
and the determination of the directions should be made in accordance with it. / The lord god Saṃkarṣaṇa should be made facing the southern direction;
and next, Pradyumna should be made facing the western direction; / Aniruddha, O king, is prescribed facing the northern direction.

exceedingly damaged, coarse, frequented by wicked persons, / or that adorned with sesamum-seed spots, covered with variegated dots,
confused with lines and circles, pierced, or combined with vimala mineral. / Vimala should be known as threefold: derived from iron, from bronze, and from gold.
That which is attended by iron vimala causes the destruction of people; / that possessing bronze-like vimala destroys people’s honor;
that combined with golden vimala brings famine as well as drought. / If a circle is seen upon rubbing and cutting,
one should know that [stone] to have a fetus, and should carefully avoid it. / When the womb is resembling madder-red, there is a frog inside;
in a yellowish circle, a monitor lizard; in a black one, he should know there is a snake; / in a tawny one, he should know there is a mouse; in a reddish one, a chameleon;
in a molasses-colored one, a stone; in a dove-colored one, a gecko; / water in one looking like the color of a sword; and sand in an ash-colored one.
Hence, if joined with even one of these, it leads to the ruin of the patron. / Thus stones with fetuses should be known through their external marks.

When the whitewashing is finished, the work of painting is prescribed: / the plinth should be well painted, and the back should be made white with plaster.
It should be executed in accordance with the rules of painting, granting the fulfilment of all desires; / and other marvels should be diligently depicted there.
As the emblem in the temple of Viṣṇu, one should make Garuḍa; / in the temple of Durgā, a lion; and a trident in the dwelling of Hara;
a lotus should be the emblem in the temple of Lakṣmī, a swan in the temple of Brahmā, / a makara for Kāmadeva, and a palm tree for Saṃkarṣaṇa;
a lion standing upon a crescent moon should be in the temple of Āditya; / for the moon, it should be a deer standing inside a circular disc;
one should make a vajra for Śakra, and a staff for Vaivasvata, / likewise a noose for Varuṇa, and indeed a club for Dhanada;
a spear should be made for Skanda, and for Gaṇādhyakṣa, O king, / an axe should be made as the emblem, [while] flame is prescribed for Vahni;

3.98
Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Now, the characteristics of the temple [precinct]:
In front, in an utterly clean area of ground, one should prepare three mounds / using clay appropriate to the caste along with sand.
Then one should smear the three mounds with fresh cow dung.
In the middle among them is the preliminary consecration mound;
to its south is the worship mound;
to its north is the fire-offering mound.
There, on the preliminary consecration mound, four archways are constructed.
All the mounds are square, four cubits in height, and wide as a third of an archway.
One should surround the two mounds with fresh flags and protective cords.
Having brought wood for the archways from any of the auspicious trees according to the rite prescribed for entering the forest, one should set them up for the mounds.
One must not make an archway from two pieces of wood,
nor should it be stunted;
and all archways must be of equal measure.
At the very beginning, at the western part of the preliminary consecration mound, facing east, the ritual director should not be the owner.
To the south of the ritual director, facing east indeed, should be the Sāttvata;
to his south as well, facing east indeed, should be the knower of the Ṛgveda.
To the left of the ritual director, facing east indeed, is the sacrificer;
to his left, facing east indeed, is the arghabāhu; stationed at the mound, facing south, is the knower of the Yajurveda.
At the mound, in the east, facing west, is the knower of the Sāmaveda;
to his south is the flute player;
to his south as well is the reciter;
to his north is the singer;
behind are the players of stringed instruments.
To the south of the mound, facing north, is the knower of the Atharvaveda.
At the fire-offering mound, facing east, to the west of the fire-shed, is the invoker.
[The shed] is square,
measuring one cubit on all sides, and the fire-shed has stepped tiers; to its north are the guardian of mantras and the guardian of materials. At the worship mound is the Sāttvata who performs the worship;
to his south, facing north, are the guardian of mantras and the guardian of materials.
The astrologer, in another place, possessed of unique knowledge, should be attentive to catching the [auspicious] moment.
For the architect, there is no restriction.

Thus ends the hundred and first chapter, entitled 'Description of the Preliminary Consecration', in the third book of the venerable Viṣṇudharmottara, in the dialogue between Mārkaṇḍeya and Vajra.
3.102
Mārkaṇḍeya said:
I invoke the living soul, the seed, the all-pervading Lord, / established in five states in the body, and established again in a fivefold manner;
established again in a fivefold way through the nature of wind, / and likewise established in a fivefold way through the nature of fire;
the Lord established in a fivefold way through the nature of water, / and established again in a fivefold way as the essence of the elements;
the Supreme Self, extremely fierce and gentle, the Lord of the world, / all-pervading, supporter of all, Lord of all, unconquered;
infinitely subtle, the boon-giver, the knower of the field, the sovereign Lord, / the Lord of the past, present, and future, bowed to by the worlds;
the substrate of qualities, the womb of the world, greater than the great. / O divine living soul, come forth; enter this image yourself;

may all the earth-supporting mountains arrive for me, lords of immobile things. / I will invoke the trees possessing various forms;
may all the trees arrive, having the swiftness of the mind and the wind. / I will likewise invoke the nether worlds accessible to all;
may all the nether worlds arrive, blameless abodes of the Daityas. / I will invoke the continents, all sustaining marvels;
may the continents arrive, which serve as dwelling places for worlds and are dear to the world. / I will invoke the worlds, delightful dwelling places;
may all the worlds arrive for me, creators of living beings. / I will likewise invoke the oceans dear to the world;
may the oceans arrive for me, repositories of water and dear to the world. / I will invoke here the regions of the earth, shelters of all beings;
and may the regions of the earth, delightful to all, also come here. / I will likewise invoke the four seas;
may the seas arrive swiftly for me, all holding water. / And I will invoke the rivers, boon-granting mothers of the world;

may the boon-granting rivers arrive, auspicious and removing all sins. / I will likewise invoke all the hosts of clouds;
may all the highly fortunate, swift-moving clouds arrive for me. / I will likewise invoke the all-pervading Piśācas; / may all the Piśācas arrive for me, taking form at will.
I will likewise invoke the companions of Skanda;
may the multiform companions of Skanda arrive for me. / I will likewise invoke the attendants of the gods;
may the multiform attendants of the gods arrive for me. / I will likewise invoke the attendants of Hara; / may the multiform attendants of Hara arrive for me.
I will likewise invoke the Mothers, the mothers of the world;
may the divine Mothers arrive, boon-granting, moving everywhere, auspicious. / I will invoke the planets, lords of all the worlds;
may all the radiant planets arrive, lords of the universe. / I will likewise invoke the boon-granting constellations;

Mārkaṇḍeya said:
One should worship the fourfold form of the Lord, which grants all desires;
a man desiring dharma should always worship Aniruddha.

Praising the Lotus-eyed at Puṣkara, the Mace-bearer at Gayā,
and likewise Viṣṇu at Lohadaṇḍa, one crosses beyond misdeeds.
Praising me as Raghava at Citrakūṭa, as the Slayer of Daityas at Prabhāsa,
and as Govinda at Vṛndāvana, one becomes an inheritor of merit.
Praising Jaya at Jayantī, and Jayanta at Hastināpura,
Varāha at Kandamāla, and the Discus-bearer in Kaśmīra;
as Janārdana at Kubjāmra, and Keśava at Mathurā,
likewise Śrīdhara at Śrīkubja, and the Foremost of Gods at Gaṅgādvāra;
as Mahāyogya at Śāligrāma, Hari on Mount Govardhana,
the Four-armed at Piṇḍāraka, and the Conch-bearer at Śaṅkhadvāra;
as Vāmana at Kurukṣetra, and Trivikrama on the Yamunā,
likewise Viśveśvara on the Śoṇa, and Kapila at the eastern ocean;
and the Lord of Śvetadvīpa at the confluence of the Gaṅgā and the ocean,
the Bearer of the Earth on the bank of the Devikā, Yogaśāyin at Prayāga;

named Nara and Nārāyaṇa at the hermitage of Badarī,
and by the name Padmanābha at the southern ocean, O Arjuna;
likewise praising Kṛṣṇa at Dvārakā, one crosses beyond misfortune;
in this way, [praising] Rāma on Mount Mahendra, and likewise Hṛṣīkeśa, O wise one;
Hayagrīva at Aśvatīrtha, the Universal Form on the Himālaya,
Nṛsiṃha at Kṛtaśauca, and the Friend of the Twice-born on the Vipāśā;

Having properly fasted on the third day of the bright fortnight of Jyeṣṭha,
a man should perform the worship of the earth with incense, garlands, and unguents,
with lamps and diverse foods, and he should give a sacrificial fee to brahmins:
vessels filled with seeds, along with gold, according to his means.
Thus the worship of the intermediate space is prescribed in Āṣāḍha;
a sacrificial fee of a pair of garments should be given there to a twice-born.
And the worship of heaven should be done in Śrāvaṇa just as before;
and there he should bestow as a sacrificial fee an umbrella and a pair of sandals.
Thus, in three months, the first breaking of the fast takes place here;
and another in three months, and again after that in three months;
then another in three months: in a year, O sinless one,
a set of four breakings of fast is to be understood for one constant in dharma.
Having performed the first breaking of the fast, he obtains the fruit of the Agniṣṭoma;
likewise, O king, at the second breaking of the fast, the fruit of the Atirātra.
At the third breaking of the fast, the wise man obtains the fruit of the Vājapeya;
at the fourth breaking of the fast, that which is declared the fruit of the Rājasūya.

Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Formerly, in the Kṛta age, the people were established in knowledge, O king;
because people were established in knowledge then, O scion of the Yadu race,

3.230
The Swan said:
[The food] of cultivators, imprisoned thieves, eunuchs, actors, / fallen persons, outcasts, villains, and prison guards;
of menstruating women, childless widows, heretics, executioners, / physicians, hunters, unchaste women, enemies of the king;
of leather-workers, professional warriors, weapon sellers, / washermen, cruel men, the afflicted, those ruled by women, communal priests;
of tailors, usurers, forgers, and those who sacrifice for śūdras— / the food of these must not be eaten, nor of anyone who offers it without respect.
Food distributed by turn, sneezed upon, proclaimed aloud, looked at by an outcaste, / given as a fee, stale, leftover, as well as [the food] of one who lives on leftovers;
and that touched by a menstruating woman, that smelled by a cow or a dog, / fallen upon by hair or insects, and likewise eaten of by a bird;
the food of a slave, a sharecropper, a cowherd, and a barber—though not of others— / as well as the food of a śūdra, ancestral offerings, and [the food] of one who sells himself.
Stale food may be eaten if smeared with fat, like seasoned dishes, / and even without fat, wheat, barley, matured grains, and nimba fruits.

Except for that of goats, cows, and buffaloes, all milks [are forbidden], / and even theirs within ten days of calving, and that of a cow whose calf has died.
The kapiñjala partridge, the tittiri partridge, the peacock, and the quail— / these birds are indeed edible, but never the rest.
The hare, the porcupine, the monitor lizard, the rhinoceros, and likewise the turtle: / they declare these edible among five-toed animals, but those with teeth in both jaws are inedible.
All single-hoofed animals must be avoided, as well as known animals and birds [not prescribed], / and garlic, carrot, onion, and mushrooms;
and red tree-resins, the śigru, and the śleṣmātaka; / and pieces of hooves are likewise inedible for the twice-born;

Slaying a cow, sacrificing for those unfit to sacrifice, adultery, selling oneself, / abandoning a guru, mother, Vedic study, the sacred fire, or a son;
being a younger brother marrying before the elder, marrying before an elder brother, / giving a maiden to either of these two, and performing sacrifices for either of them;
defiling a maiden, usury, breaking a vow, / and selling a pond, sacred knowledge, a wife, or offspring;
becoming an outcaste, abandoning relatives, teaching for a fee, / receiving instruction from one paid for teaching, and selling forbidden goods;
taking superintendence over all works, operating large machines, / living by harmful herbs or women, sorcery, and root-magic;
cutting down unseasoned trees for firewood, / undertaking actions for one's own sake, and eating condemned food;
not maintaining the sacred fires, theft, non-payment of debts, / studying improper treatises, and the practice of acting or singing;
stealing grain, base metals, or cattle, consorting with women who drink liquor, / slaying a woman, a śūdra, a vaiśya, or a kṣatriya, and atheism—these are secondary sins.

Destruction of sin is to be achieved by ascetic austerity; growth of dharma is to be achieved by ascetic austerity;
upon the destruction of sin and the growth of dharma, a man's desires are fulfilled, O brahmins.
3.237
The sages said:
By which action does a person obtain what, O wise one?
Sever this doubt of ours, who are twice-borns, O best of the twice-born.
The Haṃsa said:
By giving gifts one becomes enjoyer of pleasures; by ascetic austerity one gains splendor;
by serving elders, O brahmins, a person becomes wise.
By sacrifice one attains worlds, and destruction of sin by oblations;
by recitation one attains desires, and by truth the supreme state.
By bathing one attains purity, especially from breath-control;
by meditation and concentrations one attains the unsurpassed abode.
By self-restraint one attains everything, whatever one wishes in the mind;
by purity the gods are pleased, and they are pleased by fasting.
For those purified by fasting, attainment of desires is certain;
[action] grants extensive enjoyments to those who do not flee in battles.

3.264
Haṃsa said:
A man endowed with self-control will attain all desires;
therefore, self-control should be exercised with all effort by one who knows.
Subjugation of the senses is called self-control in this world;
no rites of one who is not self-controlled are fruitful here, O best of twice-born.
One who is not self-controlled does not attain success, nor a desired destination;
one who is not self-controlled does not attain fortune, nor indeed anything at all.
Of a mortal with five senses, if even a single sense is breached,
then his wisdom leaks away, like water from a leather vessel.
What wise man would abandon the aims of human life and constantly strive
for the sake of the senses, when striving for his own true interest?
Through attachment to the senses, one goes to a terrible destination;
conquering these very senses, a man attains all desires.
The self-controlled man goes to heaven; the self-controlled man goes on the path of liberation;
for the self-controlled there is no grief at all; the self-controlled attains indeed all desires.

Thus ends the two hundred and sixty-fourth chapter, entitled 'Description of the Praise of Self-Control,' in the Haṃsagītā addressed to the sages, in the dialogue between Mārkaṇḍeya and Vajra, in the third book of the glorious Viṣṇudharmottara.
3.265
Haṃsa said:
Truth alone is the supreme brahman; truth alone is the supreme austerity;
truth alone is the supreme sacrifice; truth alone is the supreme sacred learning.
Truth keeps watch in the Vedas; release is the fruit of the tree of truth;
[so too are] austerity, renown, merit, and the worship of ancestors, gods, and seers.
The primordial ordinance and knowledge—everything is established upon truth;
truth is the sacrifices, as well as the Vedas, the mantras, and the goddess Sarasvatī.
So too the observance of vows is truth, and the syllable Oṃ is truth alone;
by truth the wind blows; by truth the sun shines;
by truth fire burns; by truth one goes to heaven;
and by truth Parjanya pours waters upon the surface of the earth.
Reciting the entirety of all the Vedas, bathing in all sacred fords,
and speaking the truth in the world—might these two be equal, or are they not equal?

A thousand horse-sacrifices and truth were held on a scale;
truth alone outweighs a thousand horse-sacrifices.
By truth the gods are pleased, as are the ancestors and brahmins;
they declare truth to be the highest dharma; therefore, one should not transgress truth.
The sages are devoted to truth; the foremost of the gods are established in truth;
always intent on truth, they rejoice in heaven like divinities.
And they travel in celestial chariots crowded with hosts of apsarases;
truth should indeed always be spoken; there is nothing higher than truth.
In the unfathomable, spotless, pure pool of purity whose water is truth,
one should bathe in the mental sacred ford; that bathing is considered supreme.
Whether for their own sake, or for another's sake, or for the sake of a son, or on account of an enemy,
those who do not speak untruth—those men go to heaven.
That which is exceedingly beneficial to living beings is truth—such is the conviction;
whatever would be beneficial to living beings, that very thing should be spoken by one who knows.

On this subject also, the unfallen cite this ancient verse: / 'If you have no dispute with yourself and with King Yama, the son of Vivasvat, who dwells in your heart, / do not go to the Gaṅgā, nor go to Kurukṣetra.'
One should continually perform action that gives lightness to the mind; / all the meritorious deeds of such a person become manifold,
whichever [deeds] a mortal endowed with purity of mind performs. / The mind alone reveals the initial forms of a man;
the mind alone knows the heaviness and lightness of an action. / Truth is purity, self-restraint is purity, and bodily cleanliness is purity;
all purities belong to him whose mind is always pure; / for one bereft of mental purity, all purities are useless.
Yet it cannot be made artificially by those devoid of faithlessness.
He whose mind is ever free from defect and subject to virtue,
he alone sacrifices with sacrifices; he alone performs asceticism;
he alone attains all whatever he desires in his mind.
Therefore, mental purity is declared superior to all purities.

and bringing both hands across to the base of the thighs, even
and turned upward, O foremost of the twice-born, placing the right above the left,
and the hands should be positioned with the thumbs slightly bent;
raising [the body] thereby and keeping the arms slightly loose without strain,
the wise man should align the spine with the shoulders and elevate the body;
the neck should be made motionless and very firm, and the head held level.
Looking at the tip of his nose and not looking at the directions,
closing the eyes slightly, he should cast off all that is external;
renouncing all that is external, [he should behold] the Supreme Self with the self,
in the heart-lotus, in the center of the pericarp, splendour deposited in splendour;
having beheld the Lord, the Cosmic Person, he is freed from all bonds.
Having first restrained the mind through withdrawal,
a man who has gained a target through concentration ascends to meditation.

Having conquered the senses, having controlled the mind,
and having likewise conquered the breath, a man ascends to meditation;
mounted on meditation, he attains that state having gone to which he grieves no more.
At the end of the night and at twilight, effort must be made by a man in meditation;
having seen the Supreme Cosmic Person through meditation, freed from the body, he attains wholeness.
3.284
The Haṃsa said:
Now, having mounted meditation, he should attain samādhi of the mind;
having attained samādhi, a man should not think of anything thereafter.

By serving cows here, O chiefs of the twice-born, one attains worlds that are spotless and free from sorrow;
therefore, service to cows must be performed with effort by men intent on dharma.
Haṃsa said:
He who is compassionate is a true man; the pitiless one is considered a rākṣasa.
Even rākṣasas possess pity, O best of the twice-born.
The merciless go straight to a terrible hell;
then, in human birth, they suffer killing, imprisonment, and torment.
They become afflicted with illness, miserable, wretched, and poor;
[while] compassionate men, having reached heaven, shine brilliantly.
Therefore a wise man should be compassionate, even toward blades of grass;
even blades of grass are endowed with life; one should not inflict pain on them.
Compassion is to be shown to an insect, a winged creature, a domestic animal, and likewise to a deer;
to the highest class, to the one devoid of class, to an outcaste, and to one of mixed class.
Compassion is to be practiced everywhere; it grants the fruit of all desires;
even among all vows, the single vow of compassion is supreme.

at Gaṅgādvāra, Kuśāvarta, Bilvaka, Nīlaparvata, / likewise at the Kanakhala pilgrimage place, and likewise Śāligrāma;
at Vārāṇasī, at the Sarasvatī, at the confluence of the Gaṅgā and the ocean, / at the meeting of the Sindhu and the ocean, and at the Tritakūpa, O sinless ones;
at the holy Plakṣaprasravaṇa, and at Camasonmajjana; / at Mahālaya, Kedāra, and likewise Bhṛgutuṅga;
at the holy Mānasa, and likewise Uttaramānasa; / in these and other pilgrimage places with sacred waters that may exist,
and on the banks of rivers, it should be known to bear abundant fruit. / Likewise, what is given to the poor, the blind, the wretched, the helpless, the mute,
the disabled and others—the dull-witted, the dwarf, the lame— / and to those afflicted with disease, that wealth yields abundant fruit.
What is given as aid for dharma, when a sacrifice is being performed, / or for solemnizing a marriage, is proclaimed imperishable.
A man through whose wealth received someone enters into festive joy / becomes a sharer in festivals wherever he is born.

having risen early in the morning, should gratify the brahmins and the two Aśvins;
he attains great beauty and is also freed from diseases.
Thus the gift of cows and the products of cows yields great fruit.

As many hairs as there are on the cow, so much reward the giver of the cow obtains;
and he rescues his sons, grandsons, and entire family up to the seventh generation in the other world.
Haṃsa said:
Understand, O best of the twice-born, the gift of a ghee-cow:
one should prepare a pot filled with clarified butter of cow-milk;
and likewise one should prepare a full water jar as her calf,
covered with a bronze lid, accompanied by white cloth,
both containing gold within and smeared on the outside;
her legs made of sugarcane stalks, and the hooves made of silver;
the two eyes should be made of gold, and the two horns made of aloe-wood;
and seven grains should be prepared placed separately on her sides;
her cloth should be of silk, and her blanket [also], O best of the twice-born;
one should place frankincense far on her breath, and her udder made of gold;
likewise the tongue made of sugar, the face made of molasses and milk,
the tail of flax-thread, and the hairs of white mustard seeds;

likewise of spiced rice under Ārdrā, and of silver under [the asterism of] Aditi; / of clarified butter under Puṣya, and of perfumes under the serpent-asterism;
of sesame seeds under the conjunction of Maghā, of panic seed under the deity Bhaga; / of sweet cakes under the asterism of Aryaman, and of milk-rice under that of Savitṛ;
of multicoloured garments under Citrā, of roasted grain flour under the deity Vāyu; / of metals under [the asterism of] Indra and Agni, and of a garland and fruit under that of Mitra;
likewise of an umbrella under Śāka, and of roots and fruits under Mūla; / the gift of snow with honey under the asterism of the Waters yields great fruit;
of food under [the asterism of] the Viśvedevas, and of a garment under Śravaṇa; / of grain under [that of] the Vasus, O brahmins, and of medicine under that of Varuṇa;
of seeds under [the asterism of] Aja Ekapād, and immediately after, of crops; / likewise of cow's milk products under that of Pūṣan, and of baths under that of the Aśvins;
and the gift of sesame seeds under the Bharaṇīs bears great fruit. / Next, on the first lunar day, of flowers, and on the second, of clarified butter;

on the third, of garments, and on the fourth, of gold; / on the fifth, of fruits, and on the sixth, of a bath, O honour-givers;
and on the seventh, of sweet cakes, and on the eighth, of molasses; / on the ninth, of gruel, and on the tenth, of silver;
on the eleventh, of gold, and on the twelfth, of clothing; / on the thirteenth, of fragrant substances, and immediately after, of white sugar;

He should please the horse-headed deity [Hayaśiras] by giving a horse in Āśvina; / he attains the world of Viṣṇu and elevates his lineage.
In Kārttika, by giving a woolen garment, he attains heaven. / The gift of salt in Mārgaśīrṣa brings great reward.
Likewise [the gift] of grains in Pauṣa, and of firewood in the following [month, Māgha]; / and of all perfumes in Phālguna; here no doubt should be entertained.
The Caitra twelfth conjoined with the asterism Bhāgya (Pūrvaphālgunī) brings great reward; / the Vaiśākhī joined with Hasta; likewise in Jyeṣṭha with Svātī;
and in Āṣāḍha with Jyeṣṭhā; and in the month of Śrāvaṇa with Mūla; / likewise in the month of Bhādrapada joined with Śravaṇa;
in Āśvina the holy twelfth day is conjoined with the Aja asterism (Pūrvabhādrapadā); / in Kārttika joined with Revatī; and in the month of Mārgaśīrṣa with Kṛttikā;
in Pauṣa conjoined with Mṛgaśiras; in Māgha joined with the asterism of Āditya (Punarvasu); / and in Phālguna conjoined with Puṣya: such a twelfth day is supremely purifying.
When fasting on these [twelfth days] conjoined with these asterisms, the gift [then given], / O best of the twice-born, is known to yield the fulfillment of all desires and to be endless.

At the transit of the sun into Aries, the gift of a ram brings great reward. / At the transit into Taurus, the gift of cattle is likewise proclaimed;
and the gift of a bed and a seat when the sun enters Gemini. / At the entrance into Cancer, [the gift] of roasted barley flour and sugar candy,
and of molasses, treacle, candy, and white sugar, O twice-born, / and of salt, clarified butter, and fruit, as well as green herbs;
and likewise the gift of water jars is known to yield great reward. / At the entrance into Leo, [the gift] of vessels made of metal;
at the entrance into Virgo, of fragrant garments; / at the entrance into Libra, [the gift] of grains and seeds is excellent;
at the entrance into Scorpio, the gift of garments and dwellings; / at the entrance into Sagittarius, [the gift] of garments and vehicles yields great reward;
at the entrance into Capricorn, the gift of firewood and fire; / at the entrance into Aquarius, the gift of grass for cattle; / at the entrance into Pisces, [the gift] of dwelling places and garlands is best.

When that is established, he attains success; otherwise, the reverse. / Without having cleared away an accusation, one may not counter-charge him;
yet one may bring a counter-charge in a brawl or in acts of violence. / For both, sureties capable of determining the case should be taken.
In violence, theft, defamation, assault, and in the case of a woman, / he should conduct the litigation immediately; elsewhere, time is declared to be at discretion.
He shifts from place to place and repeatedly licks the corners of his mouth; / his forehead perspires, and his face loses its colour;
his mouth drying up and his speech faltering, he talks much that is contradictory: / whether in an accusation or in giving testimony, such a man is declared corrupt.
By appearances, gestures, gait, bodily actions, and speech, / and by the alterations of the eyes and face, the inner mind is grasped.
One who does not give a reply is to be known as defeated after three fortnights. / In all disputes, the later transaction is stronger;
but in the case of a pledge, an acceptance of gifts, or a purchase, the earlier one is stronger. / Against one who causes all trouble, when proved guilty in one part,

Thus ends the three hundred and twenty-eighth chapter, entitled "Ascertainment of the Divine Ordeal," in the Haṃsagītā addressed to the sages, in the dialogue between Mārkaṇḍeya and Vajra, in the third book of the glorious Viṣṇudharmottara.
3.329
Haṃsa said:
One, two, and likewise three, and four in order— / these are declared by the wise to be the wives of śūdras, vaiśyas, kṣatriyas, and brahmins.
Only a śūdra woman is remembered as the wife of a śūdra; that [śūdra woman] and his own are for a vaiśya; / those [two] and his own are for a king; and those [three] and his own are for the first-born.
A woman of a different caste should never be the [chief] wife of anyone; / only a woman of the same caste should be the senior wife, even when women of other castes are present.
Eight marriages are taught; I shall speak of their characteristics. / The Brāhma is [the giving of a maiden] after summoning; the Daiva is said to be [giving her] to officiating priests;
the Ārṣa is from receiving a pair of cattle; the Prājāpatya is likewise when requested; / the Āsura is from accepting wealth; the Gāndharva is by mutual consent of the two;
the Rākṣasa is by abduction in battle; the Paiśāca is by tricking the maiden. / The Prājāpatya, the Daiva, and the Brāhma—these three are auspicious;

the king too must cause the funerary ball to be offered for him through his servants according to rule.
Whosoever among men here is the recipient of the wealth is likewise declared to be the recipient of the debt;
and after always using what remains after the debt, O foremost of the twice-born, a man would ever be righteous.

At the end of the night, the herdsman should lead the cattle to the forest;
satisfied and having drunk water, he should deliver them all in the evening.
By day, responsibility lies with the herdsman; at night, with the owner in his house.
In the event of a mishap to the cattle, the cowherd should exert himself there according to his capacity.
Even if they fall when he is unable [to save them], he should report that to the owner;
if unable to fight [the danger], he should report it to the owner, raising a cry of distress.
The cowherd, however, must bear punishment before the king
for what is lost, ruined by worms, killed by dogs, or died in an accident,
if lacking in human effort—the herdsman must deliver [its remains].
When goats and sheep are trapped by wolves, and the herdsman does not come,
whatever [beast] a wolf kills by force, that guilt falls upon the herdsman;
if, overtaken by fate while grazing together in the forest,
a wolf pounces upon and kills one, the herdsman bears no guilt in that case.
What is stolen by thieves after being fought off, the herdsman ought not to make good,
if he announces it to the owner at the proper place and time.
By this, the dispute concerning all herdsmen has been declared.

sleeping in the open air during the rains, and eating only at night;
or he may eat at the fourth mealtime, or indeed at the sixth mealtime;
or he may pass the time through penances, and maintain celibacy.
Passing his time in the forest in this way, the twice-born becomes an inhabitant of the hermitage of Brahmā.
Relinquishing the bodily elements in the forest, he attains the worlds of the Great-grandfather;
and he purifies seven past generations and seven future ones, O brahmins.
3.340
Haṃsa said:
One should dwell during the fourth, final portion of one's life in the order of renouncers,
after duly performing an offering endowed with all one's possessions as sacrificial fee.
Having deposited the fires within himself, the twice-born should enter the holy order;
he should stay one night in a village and five nights in a city.
When the smoke has cleared, the pestle is set down, the embers are extinguished, and people have finished eating,
when the movement of vessels has ceased, the renouncer should continually go about for alms.
He should beg alms from seven houses, and not beg food after another beggar;
he should not be dismayed at obtaining nothing, subsisting on whatever is obtained.

The mendicant should not relish flavors, nor should he eat in anyone's house;
his vessels should be earthen, of gourd, of wood, or of split bamboo;
their purification is declared to be through scrubbing and through water.
He should take as clothing to cover his loincloth what is cast away on the road.
His dwelling should be an empty house; where evening overtakes the sage, there he is a householder;
he should apply restraint of action, restraint of mind, and restraint of speech.
He should place his foot purified by sight; he should drink water purified by cloth;
he should speak words purified by truth; he should perform action purified by the mind.
If one [man] anoints his arm with sandalwood, or if another hacks it,
he should not think of either good or ill concerning either of them.
Through breath-controls he should burn away defects; through concentrations, sin;
through sensory withdrawal, attachments; through meditation, qualities devoid of the Lord.
Free from desires, paying homage to none, viewing a clod of earth, a stone, and gold as equal,
impartial toward all beings, friendly, he attains the supreme state.

3.352
Nara and Nārāyaṇa said:
First of all, in a charming place, any one among the vicinity of a lake, a river, a spring, a house, a garden, or a mountain peak, one should prepare a very level, delightful sacred diagram measuring a cubit.
In its middle, one should draw an eight-petaled lotus, and then divide it with the described four-sided, four-gated line.
Then in the middle of the lotus pericarp, one should meditate on a white hundred-petaled lotus.
Above its pericarp is the solar disc, above that the lunar disc, above that the fire-disc; in its middle one should ritually place the supreme Cosmic Person, incorporeal, odourless, tasteless, formless, without touch, soundless, omnipresent, ending in a prolated syllable, the Oṃkāra.
On the eastern petal of the lotus one should ritually place the syllable "A", Vāsudeva, and meditate on him as white-coloured; on the southern petal one should ritually place the syllable "Ā", Saṃkarṣaṇa, and meditate on him shining like a lotus petal;
on the western petal, on the syllable "Oṃ", one should ritually place Pradyumna, and meditate on him as yellow-coloured;
on the northern petal one should ritually place the syllable "A", Aniruddha, and meditate on him as black-coloured;
on the north-eastern petal one should ritually place Brahmā with "Tat", and meditate on him shining like a lotus petal;
one should meditate on him as equal to ten million suns;
on the north-western petal one should ritually place Varāha;
one should meditate on him as equal to crushed collyrium;
at the eastern gate one should ritually place Vainateya with these syllables: "Oṃ Keṃ Ṭeṃ Yeṃ";
and one should meditate on him as golden-coloured;
at the southern gate one should ritually place Sudarśana, the chariot-wheel of splendour;
one should meditate on it as having a diamond hub, a thousand spokes, and splendour equal to ten million suns;
at the western gate one should ritually place the Śārṅga bow;
one should meditate on it as resembling Indra's bow;
at the northern gate one should ritually place Aniruddha with "Sveṃ Vai Ṭheṃ Heṃ Theṃ";
one should meditate on him as having all colours;
in the intermediate directions one should ritually place the conch Pāñcajanya with "Veṃ Ṇeṃ Kṣeṃ Kṣeṃ";
one should meditate on it as the colour of the moon;
one should ritually place Śrī, the lotus, to the south with "Gheṃ Heṃ Bheṃ Heṃ";
one should meditate on her as white;
one should ritually place Puṣṭi within the lotus with "Geṃ Ḍeṃ Veṃ Śeṃ";
one should meditate on her as white;
one should ritually place the forest garland in front with "Heṃ Seṃ";
one should meditate on it with "Heṃ Seṃ" as multi-coloured;
in the north-east one should ritually place the Nandaka sword with "Oṃ Cit";
one should meditate on it as the colour of space;
in the south-east one should ritually place the armour with "Oṃ M";
one should meditate on it as multi-coloured;
one should ritually place the Śrīvatsa mark with "Oṃ Kṣeṃ Leṃ";
one should meditate on it as white;
in the north-west one should ritually place the Kaustubha jewel with "Oṃ Cheṃ Ṭheṃ", and meditate on it as a blazing sun;
then in the sacred diagram, in their respective directions on the outside, one should ritually place the lords of the directions with the syllables "Oṃ" and "Auṃ": Śakra and Agni, Yama, Nairṛta, Varuṇa, Pavana, Kubera, and in the north-east [Īśāna];
one should meditate on them according to their described forms;
with the twelve-syllable mantra ending with "Oṃ namo bhagavate vāsudevāya", one should offer water for arghya, water for the feet, water for sipping, ointment, flowers, a lamp, incense, honey-mixture, food-offering, fruit, and edibles to the Cosmic Person in the middle of the pericarp; or with the eight-syllable mantra, "Oṃ namo nārāyaṇāya";
[or] with this mantra: "Oṃ, victory to You, O Lotus-eyed One! Homage to You, O Creator of the Universe! / Homage be to You, O Hṛṣīkeśa, O Great Cosmic Person, O First-born!"
or with the Puruṣasūkta;
then one should worship the other deities seated on the petals individually with the stated mantras.
That man who worships Viṣṇu according to this rite / accomplishes all aims and indeed becomes one who has achieved his goal.
